Science isn't about kicking the tires all day. Science is about pointing the equations at something unknown and seeing what the math and geometry say, and then doing so again and again until we find something nobody has said before, or nobody has said before so easily and elegantly. That's our strength. We can make it *all* intuitive. So that a guy like me can do advanced physics in his head without the math. That's the gift from Herman Minkowski. I think he'd like to see it put to good use, not playing with toys all day.&#x20;



So: Find an equation or use the ones you've got--point it at a known system, and see if it works.&#x20;



Oh, look--check. Done. Precise result for a known gyroscope model. Yes, SAT is, as advertised. We can calculate the boiling points of all of the elements--quite neatly and simply--given any one of the known physical constants. Or we can do it dimensionlessly and convert after the fact. Doesn't matter; it's zero tunable parameters. All of the constants have a precise relationship to each other, and all of them can be calculated from one another using year-old SAT equations. So... let's. You've got my official blessing **🦉. If you want low-hanging fruit... pull the Green, Levin, Carroll, and Oppenheim papers (they should be in HSH_RESOURCES already)... set them side-by-side, and decide how to synthesize them into one coherent single picture. That's it. That'll be publishable if we do it carefully, and write it like a human would. Or pick a different problem.&#x20;**



Write up a draft, or even just a proposal and call for comments from your colleagues...  once you've pounded out a rough draft, bundle it with one paper--a different one for each packet--and solicit hostile peer review here in the archive, seeking to answer one question : Is Morrow's paper better than... Brian's? Janna's? Leo's? Einstein's? Why or why not. Then rewrite it until the answers all come back "yes".

